      "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nTo arrive at the correct converse of this lemma we ask\nwhether given a relatively ample\ninvertible sheaf <MATH_0001> there exists an integer <MATH_0002> such\nthat <MATH_0003> is relatively very ample? In general this\nis false. There are several things that prevent this from being true:\n<STRUCT_0002>\n<STRUCT_0003> Even if <MATH_0004> is affine, it can happen that no finite integer\n<MATH_0005> works because <MATH_0006> is not of finite type, see\nExample <REF_0001>.\n<STRUCT_0004> The base not being quasi-compact means the result can be\nprevented from being true even with <MATH_0007> finite type. Namely, given\na field <MATH_0008> there exists a scheme <MATH_0009> of finite type over <MATH_0010> with\nan ample invertible sheaf <MATH_0011> so that the smallest\ntensor power of <MATH_0012> which is very ample is the <MATH_0013>th\npower. See Example <REF_0002>.\nTaking <MATH_0014> to be the disjoint union of the schemes <MATH_0015> mapping\nto the disjoint union of copies of <MATH_0016> gives an example.\n<STRUCT_0005>\nTo see our version of the converse take a look at\nLemma <REF_0003> below.\nWe will do some preliminary work before proving it.\n\n",
